WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.073
One fifth of seafood is thought 
to be caught&nbsp;illegally

00:00:03.073 --> 00:00:04.256
or is simply unreported.

00:00:04.256 --> 00:00:06.325
If you can't see it, you can't manage it.

00:00:06.325 --> 00:00:07.803
And I know from&nbsp;experience,

00:00:07.803 --> 00:00:10.281
you can't monitor the whole 
ocean from the decks of ships.

00:00:10.281 --> 00:00:12.312
Using GPS location&nbsp;data,

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:15.655
Global Fishing Watch built the 
first-ever live stream map

00:00:15.655 --> 00:00:18.027
to&nbsp;monitor the industrial fishing fleet.

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:20.885
When we were working with our 
partners in Japan and&nbsp;South Korea,

00:00:20.885 --> 00:00:24.725
they told us there was illegal fishing 
suspected in North Korean waters.

00:00:24.725 --> 00:00:27.069
But when we first took a 
look on our map, we could see

00:00:27.069 --> 00:00:30.987
very little fishing because those&nbsp;vessels 
were not sharing their GPS location data,

00:00:30.987 --> 00:00:32.379
as they should.

00:00:32.379 --> 00:00:33.709
We call them “dark vessels.”

00:00:33.709 --> 00:00:35.860
And&nbsp;generally, dark vessels are up to no good.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.000
So we had to turn to other sources of data.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.032
We looked&nbsp;at satellite-based radar

00:00:41.032 --> 00:00:42.033
and optical imagery,

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:43.146
and we lit that region up.

00:00:43.146 --> 00:00:46.506
We revealed&nbsp;an armada 
of almost 1,000 vessels.

00:00:46.506 --> 00:00:48.923
The techniques we used to illuminate 
what was&nbsp;happening in North Korea,

00:00:48.923 --> 00:00:50.545
we can use anywhere

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.000
and everywhere to make the invisible visible.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.676
We can end pirate fishing.

00:00:55.676 --> 00:00:58.000
And we can transform&nbsp;ocean management

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.142
for the common good of all.

